            <ArticleTitle>[Sleep disorders and addictions: Impact on quality of life and relapse vulnerability].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1164-1169</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0755-4982(16)00053-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.lpm.2016.01.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>There is a bidirectional association between sleep disorders and addiction. Sleep problems negatively impact the quality of life of subjects with addictions (psychological state, pain perception, drug use). Sleep disorders are associated with a higher risk of relapse among sujects with addiction. The association between sleep disorders and relapse may be related to poor quality of life or to a higher level of craving. Maintaining abstinence and reducing craving episodes among subjects with addiction is the first-line treatment to reduce sleep disorders. Specific interventions focused on sleep disorders are needed when sleep disorders persist during abstinence and may improve global addiction outcome.</AbstractText>
